 "is_vocab": false,
 "stimulus_html": "For the following questions, recall that the formula for interest compounded yearly is <span data-math=\"A = P{\\left( {1 + r} \\right)^t}\">A = P{\\left( {1 + r} \\right)^t}</span>, where <span data-math=\"A\">A</span> is the amount in the account after <span data-math=\"t\">&gt;t</span> years, <span data-math=\"P\">P</span> is the initial amount deposited, and <span data-math=\"r\">r</span> is the interest rate per year. However, if the account is compounded monthly, the formula changes to <span data-math=\"A = P{\\left( {1 + \\frac{r}{{12}}} \\right)^{12t}}\">A = P{\\left( {1 + \\frac{r}{{12}}} \\right)^{12t}}</span>.",
